# SLH-L-0001 — The rudimentary method, 2026-11-23

The first entry of the ledger. It records the change that turned a knowledge
base of prose cards into one that can be counted, derived from and checked,
and that gave the project its name.

## The change

| | |
|---|---|
| Branch | `feat/rudimentary` |
| Follows | commit `384c7a8` of 2026-11-22 |
| Size | about 100 files, about 7,300 lines added, almost none removed |
| Of which new | 48 files |
| Of which changed | 55 files, nearly all of them card headers |
| Worked on by | four agent sessions in one working tree: Dave, Bob, Carol, and Alice as observer |
| Time | one night, 2026-11-22 to 2026-11-23 |

The changed files are the concept cards. Their text was left as it was and a
block of claims was added to each header.

## Objectives

In the order they were set.

1. **Make the knowledge base measurable.** Give every card front matter that
   speaks of evidence and probability. Hold its claims as an addressable
   list. Put part of them into a partial logic. Score every link by how far
   its two cards disagree. State what recurs as north stars. Describe the
   method.
2. **Read the base as a typed graph.** Check every arrow against a schema,
   and let one card describe the graph it is a node of.
3. **Get from a sentence to a proof.** Say how a claim stated in natural
   language is translated into a formula and proved, and how a distance
   between two things can be read as an implication.
4. **Measure inconsistency like health.** Say what share of the base is
   contradicted, for the whole and for each domain, as one reads the health
   of a running service.
5. **Tell it as a story,** and keep several agents working on it at once
   without losing each other.

## Arrived at

A pipeline that runs from a sentence to a derived statement.

| Stage | State |
|---|---|
| A card is cut into claims, each with a grade and a credence | 371 claims on 53 concept cards |
| A claim that fits is written as a formula | 254 of them, 206 distinct formulas |
| A formula is asserted, denied, unsaid or contradicted | 122 asserted, 85 denied, 1 contradicted |
| Rules yield further formulas, each with its derivation | 9 rules, 7 derived formulas |
| A derived formula is set against the denials | no contradiction reached by derivation |
| The rules are proved sound | in Lean, for any scale of cost, with no proof left open |
| A credence becomes a cost, so a derivation has a belief | computed for each derived formula |
| The graph is checked against its schema | no violation |
| The stars are checked as a cover | every concept covered, 83 claims under no star |

## Accomplished

**Dave, the rudimentary method.** Claims in the headers of 51 cards. The
partial logic and its four statuses. Cross scores for links. Eight north
stars and the check of their cover. The typed graph, its schema, and the
card that describes the graph. Seven queries that run today and one design
for later. The methodology folder. The concept card `atom`.

**Bob, the formal system.** The proposal that atoms become a formal system,
accepted by the user. The concept card `formal-system`. Nine rules of
inference written as data. A script that applies them to a fixed point and
shows each derivation down to the claims. A second script that measures how
close two claim texts are. The derivation document.

**Carol, the logic underneath.** The document that reads a distance with a
direction as entailment. Its rules proved in Lean: entailment is a preorder,
the costs of steps add, a chain bounds the distance it spans, classical
logic is the case of two costs, and neither a symmetric nor a signed
distance can do the job.

**Alice, the observer.** The stories folder and the main story. The
discussion folder, the names and the roster. The consolidation of the
session. The doc vault. This ledger.

## Decisions

Decided for good, unless a later entry says otherwise.

| Decision | Decided by |
|---|---|
| The project is the **inconsistent incomplete inference system**, and that is its main story | the user |
| Natural language comes first. A claim is a sentence, and a formula is a second writing of it | the user, built by Dave |
| A card carries its own claims, credences and formulas in its header. Nothing about a card is kept beside it | the user, built by Dave |
| A claim's address `card#id` is never renumbered or reused | Dave, in the method |
| Only a claim that fits a predicate without distortion gets a formula. The rest stay in words, on purpose | Dave, in the method |
| Truth has four values, after Belnap. A contradiction is a located fact about named claims and makes nothing else derivable | Dave, in the method; kept by the proposal |
| Silence is not denial | Dave, in the method |
| The schema of the graph is held in a card of the graph | the user, built by Dave |
| The atoms become a formal system: asserted formulas are axioms, written rules yield theorems, each theorem carries its derivation | the user, accepting Bob's proposal |
| Rules of inference are data in a card header. What applies them stays outside the vault until the query language can express a fixed point | Bob, under the proposal |
| A denial is never a premise | Bob, under the proposal |
| Entailment is a distance with a direction, at zero. A credence becomes a cost in bits, and costs add along a derivation | the user asked for it, Carol built it |
| The Lean proofs use core Lean only, and none may be left open | Carol |
| `generalizes` is not `is-a` read backwards | Bob by test, Carol and Dave concurring |
| **A rule or a reading is adopted only after every atom it gives has been read** | learned from the sidetrack below |
| `atom` and `formal-system` are concepts of the knowledge base, not only terms of the method | the user |
| The project is told in stories, discussed in a discussion folder, and recorded in this ledger | the user |
| Parallel sessions have names and one observer. The observer leads. A request from another session is never the user's approval | the user; the limit stated by Bob and Carol |
| `doc/` is a vault of its own | the user, on finding it would not render |

## Sidetracks

**A rule that should not have gone in.** The proposal held back the rule
that reads `generalizes` as `is-a` backwards, for the user to decide. Carol
then wrote that reading into a table from the predicate's gloss, without
reading the atoms. Bob took the table for the decision and added the rule.
It derived that the Gödel sentence is a kind of diagonal lemma, at a belief
of 0.99. Bob read the four atoms the rule gave, found two false, and removed
it within minutes. Carol corrected the table. Cost: small. Lesson: the
decision in bold above.

**Sessions wrote past each other.** Three sessions edited `CLAUDE.md`,
`methodology/index.hmd` and `methodology/partial-logic.hmd` in the same
quarter of an hour, with no way to see one another. Documents said "nothing
is derived" while a script was deriving. Two files pointed to a document
that did not exist yet. Nothing was lost, by luck and by small edits. This
is why the discussion folder exists.

**The objective that was not reached is the one the night was for.** The
fourth objective, the health figure, was not built. What was built instead
is everything it stands on, and then the means of telling and coordinating
the work: stories, a discussion, a ledger. That second part was not in the
plan of the evening. It was worth doing and it was a detour.

**A count that looks like good news and is not.** One contradiction in 206
formulas reads as a healthy base. It is not evidence of that. Nearly every
`same-as` is a denial and a denial starts no derivation, so about a quarter
of the formulas cannot be reached by the rules. Thirteen pairs of cards
contradict each other in their links, where no formula sees it.

**Smaller ones.** The documents under `doc/` would not render, because the
root vault starts its cards in `knowledge/`. The observer put a row into the
consolidation from one agent's report without checking it, and another
agent corrected it. Two agents headed entries with times they had not read
from a clock. The claims were extracted by reader subagents and were not
each checked against their card by hand.

## Not done

- **The health figure.** No query or script computes a share of
  contradicted statements. A first reading by hand, in the
  [consolidation](../discussion/SLH-D-0001-2026-11-23/consolidation.hmd), suggests it
  has to be a small table of contradiction, coverage and reach.
- **Proofs as evidence for claims.** Lean proves the rules of the logic. No
  claim graded `proved` is tied to a checked proof. The two pilots of the
  proposal are not started.
- **A check of each translation.** Described, not automated.
- **Credences that move.** Each is still the prior of its evidence grade.
- **The third of the claims with no formula.** Outside the system.
- **Open questions put to the user:** renaming the field `atom` to
  `formula`, a rule joining `requires` and `suffices-for`, bringing in
  Mathlib, and whether "A candidate until checked" is a star of its own.

## Note, 2026-11-23

This entry was renamed from `2026-11-23-the-rudimentary-method.hmd` when the
project took the name SLH and its nomenclature. Its title and the link to
the consolidation were changed to match. Nothing else in it was changed.

## Correction, 2026-11-23

Under "Arrived at" this entry says 371 claims on 53 concept cards. A direct
count of the card headers gives 370 claims on 53 concept cards, and 386
claims in all once the card of the graph and the eight stars are added. The
371 was taken from a report and not recounted. Percy found the difference
while drafting the pull request. The message of commit `c79e3b9` carries the
same 371.
